steps.body:
A feed handler converts every price it receives, millions a second. Many exchange feeds send a price as an integer mantissa and a count of decimals, so 10125 with 2 decimals means 101.25. To compare prices across feeds, you put them all on one integer scale, say 8 decimals, which means multiplying by 10 to the power `8 - decimals`. Nobody should compute that power in the hot path. Build a table once, and better still, let the compiler build it.

- A `constexpr` function can run while the program compiles, when its arguments are constants. It can still run normally at run time too.
- A `constexpr` variable must get its value at compile time, or the program doesn't compile.
- `static_assert(condition, "message")` checks a constant while compiling and stops the build with your message when it's false. It costs nothing at run time.

In C++17 a `constexpr` function may use local variables, loops, and `if`, and it may fill a `std::array`, whose `operator[]` is `constexpr`. It may not use `new`, `std::vector`, `std::string`, or input and output.

```cpp
constexpr std::array<int64_t, 19> make_pow10() {
    std::array<int64_t, 19> t{};
    t[0] = 1;
    for (std::size_t i = 1; i < t.size(); ++i) {
        t[i] = t[i - 1] * 10;
    }
    return t;
}

constexpr std::array<int64_t, 19> kPow10 = make_pow10();
static_assert(kPow10[18] == 1'000'000'000'000'000'000, "10^18 fits in int64_t");
std::cout << kPow10[9] << '\n';  // 1000000000
```

The sandbox compiles at `-O0`, with no optimizer, yet `kPow10` is still plain data in the program. Calling `make_pow10()` where no `constexpr` variable asks for the result runs it at run time. At `-O2` the optimizer would likely fold that call too, but only `constexpr` guarantees it. This is g++ 10.2 at `-O0`:

```text
_ZL6kPow10:                     # the table, filled in by the compiler
        .quad   1
        .quad   10
        .quad   100
        .quad   1000
        ...
_Z12runtime_callv:              # return make_pow10()[5];
        ...
        call    _Z10make_pow10v
```

Constant evaluation also refuses undefined behavior. A first draft that stored `p` and then did `p *= 10` one time too many overflowed `int64_t` past 10^18, and g++ stopped with `error: overflow in constant expression`:

```cpp error
constexpr std::array<int64_t, 19> make_pow10() {
    std::array<int64_t, 19> t{};
    int64_t p = 1;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.size(); ++i) {
        t[i] = p;
        p *= 10;  // the last pass makes 10^19
    }
    return t;
}

constexpr std::array<int64_t, 19> kPow10 = make_pow10();
std::cout << kPow10[1] << '\n';
```

example.code:
#include <array>
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>

constexpr std::array<int64_t, 19> make_pow10() {
    std::array<int64_t, 19> t{};
    t[0] = 1;
    for (std::size_t i = 1; i < t.size(); ++i) {
        t[i] = t[i - 1] * 10;
    }
    return t;
}

constexpr std::array<int64_t, 19> kPow10 = make_pow10();
static_assert(kPow10[2] == 100, "10^2");
static_assert(kPow10[18] == 1'000'000'000'000'000'000, "10^18 fits in int64_t");

// A price sent as a mantissa and a count of decimals, on a scale of 8 decimals.
constexpr int64_t to_e8(int64_t mantissa, int decimals) {
    return mantissa * kPow10[8 - decimals];
}
static_assert(to_e8(10125, 2) == 10'125'000'000, "101.25");

int main() {
    const int64_t mantissas[] = {10125, 1012500, 7};
    const int decimals[] = {2, 4, 8};
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
        std::cout << mantissas[i] << " with " << decimals[i] << " decimals = "
                  << to_e8(mantissas[i], decimals[i]) << '\n';
    }
    std::cout << kPow10.size() << " powers, the largest " << kPow10[18] << '\n';
    return 0;
}

