steps.body:
A tick recorder writes every `Tick` to disk, and a replay tool reads them back for a backtest. The fastest way to save a `Tick` is to copy its 40 bytes with `std::memcpy(dest, src, count)` from `<cstring>`. That is only correct for a **trivially copyable** type, one whose copy is exactly a copy of its bytes. `Tick`, an `int64_t` and four `double`s, is one. A `Quote` that holds a `std::string symbol` is not: its bytes include a pointer to the string's heap buffer, and copying them is the shallow copy that ended in a double delete in the copy and move lesson.

`<type_traits>` answers such questions at compile time. The templates lesson used `std::is_integral_v<T>` with `if constexpr`. Here are three more:

- `std::is_trivially_copyable_v<T>`: copying the bytes is a correct copy.
- `std::is_arithmetic_v<T>`: `T` is an integer or floating-point type.
- `std::is_same_v<A, B>`: `A` and `B` are the same type.

Pair a trait with `static_assert`, and a template refuses the wrong type with your message. Here `std::vector<unsigned char>` is the usual byte buffer, and `buf.data()` points at its first byte:

```cpp harness
struct Tick {
    int64_t ts_ns;
    double bid, ask;
    double bid_sz, ask_sz;
};
{{code}}
std::vector<unsigned char> buf;
append_bytes(buf, Tick{34200000000000, 101.25, 101.27, 5, 3});
std::cout << buf.size() << " bytes\n";
```

```cpp
template <typename T>
void append_bytes(std::vector<unsigned char>& buf, const T& value) {
    static_assert(std::is_trivially_copyable_v<T>, "append_bytes copies raw bytes");
    const std::size_t at = buf.size();
    buf.resize(at + sizeof(T));
    std::memcpy(buf.data() + at, &value, sizeof(T));
}
```

Passing a `Quote` stops the build at the call with `error: static assertion failed: append_bytes copies raw bytes`.

The standard library asks the same question. g++ 10.2 at `-O2` compiles `std::copy` over a range of `Tick`s to one `memmove` of 40 bytes per tick, and over `Quote`s to a loop that assigns each string:

```text
_Z10copy_ticksPK4TickmPS_:      # std::copy over Ticks
        lea     rdx, [rsi+rsi*4]
        sal     rdx, 3                  # n * 40 bytes
        ...
        jmp     memmove
_Z11copy_quotesPK5QuotemPS_:    # std::copy over Quotes
        ...
.L6:
        call    _ZNSt7__cxx1112basic_stringIcSt11char_traitsIcESaIcEE9_M_assignERKS4_
        ...
        jne     .L6
```

example.code:
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <type_traits>
#include <vector>

struct Tick {
    int64_t ts_ns;          // nanoseconds since midnight
    double bid, ask;        // prices
    double bid_sz, ask_sz;  // sizes
};

struct Quote {
    std::string symbol;
    double bid, ask;
};

template <typename T>
void append_bytes(std::vector<unsigned char>& buf, const T& value) {
    static_assert(std::is_trivially_copyable_v<T>, "append_bytes copies raw bytes");
    const std::size_t at = buf.size();
    buf.resize(at + sizeof(T));
    std::memcpy(buf.data() + at, &value, sizeof(T));
}

template <typename T>
T read_bytes(const std::vector<unsigned char>& buf, std::size_t at) {
    static_assert(std::is_trivially_copyable_v<T>, "read_bytes copies raw bytes");
    T value;
    std::memcpy(&value, buf.data() + at, sizeof(T));
    return value;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    std::cout << "Tick " << sizeof(Tick) << " bytes, trivially copyable: "
              << std::is_trivially_copyable_v<Tick> << '\n';
    std::cout << "Quote " << sizeof(Quote) << " bytes, trivially copyable: "
              << std::is_trivially_copyable_v<Quote> << '\n';

    const std::vector<Tick> ticks = {{34200000000000, 101.25, 101.27, 5, 3},
                                     {34200000250000, 101.26, 101.27, 2, 4},
                                     {34200000900000, 101.24, 101.26, 7, 1}};
    std::vector<unsigned char> buf;
    for (const Tick& t : ticks) {
        append_bytes(buf, t);
    }
    std::cout << buf.size() << " bytes\n";

    const Tick second = read_bytes<Tick>(buf, 1 * sizeof(Tick));
    std::cout << second.ts_ns << ' ' << second.bid << ' ' << second.ask << ' '
              << second.bid_sz << ' ' << second.ask_sz << '\n';
    return 0;
}

