steps.body:
Prices reach a trading system in two forms: as `double`s from one feed, and as integer ticks from another (the lesson on numbers for money, later in the track, shows why integers suit money). A `mid` function should take any integer type and any floating-point type, with different code for each, because an integer price has no half tick. `std::enable_if_t` from `<type_traits>` turns a template on or off by a condition:

```cpp
template <typename T, std::enable_if_t<std::is_integral_v<T>, int> = 0>
T mid(T bid, T ask) {
    return bid + (ask - bid) / 2;  // whole ticks, rounded toward the bid
}

template <typename T, std::enable_if_t<std::is_floating_point_v<T>, int> = 0>
T mid(T bid, T ask) {
    return (bid + ask) / 2;
}
std::cout << mid(10125, 10126) << ' ' << mid(101.25, 101.26) << '\n';  // 10125 101.255
```

`std::enable_if_t<Condition, int>` is `int` when the condition is true and names no type at all when it is false. For `mid(10125, 10126)`, `T` is `int`, and the first template gets a harmless extra parameter, `int = 0`. In the second, `enable_if_t` names nothing, so substituting `int` fails, and the compiler quietly drops that candidate instead of reporting an error. The rule is called **SFINAE**: substitution failure is not an error. A type that fits neither leaves no candidate at all:

```cpp error
template <typename T, std::enable_if_t<std::is_integral_v<T>, int> = 0>
T mid(T bid, T ask) {
    return bid + (ask - bid) / 2;
}

std::cout << mid(std::string("100"), std::string("101")) << '\n';
```

g++ says `error: no matching function for call to 'mid(std::string, std::string)'`, then a note per candidate: `no type named 'type' in 'struct std::enable_if<false, int>'`.

Three tools, three jobs:

- `if constexpr` picks code inside one function.
- `static_assert` rejects a type, with your message.
- `enable_if` removes a whole overload for types that don't fit, so another overload can take them.

For two concrete types, plain overloads `mid(int64_t, int64_t)` and `mid(double, double)` are simpler. `enable_if` is for families of types. C++20 writes the same constraint with concepts, which C++17 doesn't have, so you meet `enable_if` all over C++17 libraries. g++'s own library uses it, for example, so that `std::function`, which the lesson on polymorphism costs introduces later in the track, only accepts things it can call.

example.code:
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <type_traits>

template <typename T, std::enable_if_t<std::is_integral_v<T>, int> = 0>
T mid(T bid, T ask) {
    return bid + (ask - bid) / 2;  // whole ticks, rounded toward the bid
}

template <typename T, std::enable_if_t<std::is_floating_point_v<T>, int> = 0>
T mid(T bid, T ask) {
    return (bid + ask) / 2;
}

int main() {
    const int64_t bid_ticks = 10125;
    const int64_t ask_ticks = 10126;
    std::cout << "int64_t ticks: " << mid(bid_ticks, ask_ticks) << '\n';
    std::cout << "int ticks: " << mid(99, 102) << '\n';
    std::cout << "double: " << mid(101.25, 101.26) << '\n';
    std::cout << "float: " << mid(1.5f, 2.0f) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

